# Project-Level Iteration Rules

## Purpose

These rules govern how future coding agents update this project's agent context.

## Core Invariant

The project must remain understandable, testable, and safely modifiable by future coding agents.

The project-level context must remain independent from any central bootstrap skill.

## Update Sources

Project context may be updated based on:

- user instructions
- repeated agent mistakes
- test failures
- changed commands
- changed architecture
- durable project lessons
- eval results
- review findings

## Evidence Requirement

Every durable context update should be linked to evidence:

- file path
- command output
- test failure
- user correction
- repeated pattern
- change proposal
- eval case

## Promotion Rule

A lesson may be promoted into project rules only when:

- it prevents repeated mistakes
- it clarifies architecture boundaries
- it updates stale commands
- it improves safety
- it improves testability
- the user explicitly requests it

## Risk Boundaries

Low-risk context changes:

- typo fixes
- path corrections
- command corrections
- minor clarification
- adding a documented lesson

Medium-risk context changes:

- changing workflow rules
- changing review policy
- changing eval policy
- adding new mandatory steps

High-risk context changes:

- changing project purpose
- changing architecture authority
- allowing autonomous high-risk rewrites
- removing review requirements
- deleting project history

High-risk changes require explicit user approval.

## Anti-Drift Rule

Do not let project context become:

- bloated
- contradictory
- stale
- generic
- detached from actual project behavior
- a dumping ground for temporary notes
